    {
      "index": 13,
      "case_id": "CM_NAME:14f3f71363de0ff1417e7f30e15a714310119719a0086c96310c84c5b05468be",
      "case_input_sha256": "cbc20b7e6841f7cb19ae2a8c38f0e60d3ad309ded8f7813c004ccc84cd156883",
      "decision_sha256": "29707d3f15ceb9382540f069d82f88983e576402d1f0caf64a470eb78905593a",
      "source": {
        "source": "culturemech",
        "source_case_id": "Fe(III)dicitrate",
        "source_label": "Fe(III)dicitrate",
        "raw_source_label": "Fe(III)dicitrate"
      },
      "occurrence_ids": [
        "CM_OCC:39900d9aad5fd8f761c027ee919799809654abb594caf52e62a8e7eb3dce3cb9",
        "CM_OCC:30c24431819100e1dc23ae876024e908c45be1c20c5d71a831335e1fd7394758",
        "CM_OCC:fdcf1d574c0b58aab5613d7d81833a5e4cfa7184367869e27d3f74d5331d15b7",
        "CM_OCC:d1d2d6f0771d47e307f15d90a26bd1ce161cf7bc280f7c3cf2fde8ed310c478d",
        "CM_OCC:cfc749431bbdca05b3a01c419dd2e1772366de1e065452163cd92c247a86521f",
        "CM_OCC:3b9f12c332311a93067fa7bcb8d63ed24a9d6b0db9fe77290513dc3ab0186680",
        "CM_OCC:38332d5b416420ac60e355d13084105633a0e56fab9c71751a8d66dadf2d030d",
        "CM_OCC:1f0b431219c3c37a6ed60af3a5b043f1994e64d25d0d50e0a4e4dfe0b049315f",
        "CM_OCC:1a829d3a77715091fab5bd2462b273620e18e95e9fa40609c1c4a745e059c020",
        "CM_OCC:6112f37a3bea256c90bf69dc9725cab99439d81addd66a1d790cce8d40ebac29",
        "CM_OCC:997f34ae643d552f0af7f6e3ddd89356df7b8db273431b92cd2b8e36c5148364",
        "CM_OCC:f3e7c37c5246c36e6588c25877b070a01bce71f92b2606614550c47c28b39586",
        "CM_OCC:ec0abe5c970d27c884321ae6d9c2771e44bf27cc6e8033aa70f42a6d970bfd21"
      ],
      "verdict": "accepted_precise_scientific_hold",
      "reasoning": "The 13 frozen contexts combine freshwater electron-acceptor media and BG11 derivatives. Lovley1988 p1473 says Fe(III)-citrate 20 mM; independently recovered Coppi2001 FWAFC methods say 55.9 mM Fe(III) citrate and print 13.7 g FeC6O7/L, and Risso2009 culture methods say ferric citrate 56 mM. These are source-stated ferric-citrate inputs, not evidence for the shared two-ligand CHEBI:4991 ion. Full scanned Labarre1987 pp4668–4669 and Anderson1991 pp2761–2762 were read: their methods name BG11 and modifications, but do not specify this citrate reagent there. Park2005 publisher DOI response provided abstract/references and an explicit subscription preview, not methods; its Europe PMC request timed out. Shcolnick2007 Europe PMC metadata/abstract was read, but the ScienceDirect full-article route returned HTTP403/CPE00001. The referenced Rippka1979 publisher full-text endpoint returned HTTP403/JS-cookie challenge. These are actual route-specific access limits, not a claim that all primary methods are unavailable. Hold the shared exact dicitrate association because source-to-model ligand/counterion identity has not been reconciled per occurrence; the ferric-citrate evidence already recovered must not be discarded or reclassified as an unread-method gap. Neither an unverified BG11 formulation nor one freshwater source may be applied to all13 contexts.",
      "original_decision_missing_requirement": "Recover the original methods for each frozen MediaDB occurrence and establish whether this is a modeled dicitrate complex, prepared stock, or ferric-citrate reagent; do not collapse two-citrate and one-citrate substances.",
      "missing_fact_vs_authorized_work": "Remaining fact: per-occurrence source-to-model reagent identity (ligand count, counterion and referenced BG11 formulation/lineage), not blanket recovery of all methods. Accessible Lovley/Coppi/Risso methods and Labarre/Anderson pages have now been inspected; Park subscription-preview, Shcolnick403 and Rippka403 limits remain route-specific.",
